from unit_tests import U

U('Interdependence',
 [('In the food chain grass → rabbit → fox, the rabbit is a…','primary consumer.','producer.','top predator.','It eats the producer.'),
  ('Arrows in a food chain show…','the direction energy moves.','which animal is biggest.','where animals live.','They point to the eater.'),
  ('Placing quadrats randomly helps to…','avoid bias.','count faster.','find rare plants.','Random sampling is fair.')],
 [('Give two things that plants compete for.','Light / water.~Space / minerals (nutrients).'),
  ('A 40 m² lawn is sampled with five 1 m² quadrats containing 2, 4, 3, 5 and 1 daisies. Estimate the number of daisies on the lawn.','Mean = (2 + 4 + 3 + 5 + 1) ÷ 5 = 3 per m².~3 × 40…~… = 120 daisies.'),
  ('Owls eat mice, and mice eat seeds. Explain what would happen to the seeds if the owls were removed.','The mouse population would increase.~More mice would eat more seeds.~So the number of seeds would decrease.'),
  ('A pesticide is sprayed on a field. Explain how it could harm birds of prey even though they do not eat the plants.','Small amounts of pesticide are taken in by insects or small animals eating the plants.~The pesticide is not broken down, so it builds up in their bodies.~Birds of prey eat many of these animals.~So the pesticide builds up to harmful levels in the top predator (bioaccumulation).')])

U('Speed',
 [('A runner covers 100 m in 20 s. The average speed is…','5 m/s.','2000 m/s.','0.2 m/s.','100 ÷ 20.'),
  ('On a distance–time graph, a horizontal line means the object is…','stationary.','speeding up.','moving at a steady speed.','Distance is not changing.'),
  ('Which is a typical speed for walking?','1.5 m/s.','15 m/s.','150 m/s.','About 1–2 m/s.')],
 [('A train travels at 50 m/s for 120 s. Calculate the distance travelled.','Distance = speed × time = 50 × 120.~= 6000 m.'),
  ('A cyclist travels 3 km in 10 minutes. Calculate her average speed in m/s.','3 km = 3000 m and 10 minutes = 600 s.~Speed = 3000 ÷ 600…~… = 5 m/s.'),
  ('A distance–time graph shows a straight line that becomes steeper halfway. Describe and explain the motion.','First the object moves at a steady speed.~Then it moves at a faster steady speed.~A steeper line means more distance per second.'),
  ('Describe how to find the average speed of a student running 100 m, and one way to make the timing more accurate.','Measure out 100 m with a long tape measure.~Start a stopwatch as the runner starts and stop it as they cross the line.~Calculate speed = 100 ÷ time.~Use light gates or a video to remove reaction time errors.')])

U('Gravity and forces',
 [('Weight is measured in…','newtons (N).','kilograms (kg).','metres (m).','Weight is a force.'),
  ('Forces of 30 N right and 30 N left act on a box. The box…','does not change its motion.','speeds up to the right.','speeds up to the left.','The forces are balanced.'),
  ('On the Moon, a person’s mass…','stays the same.','gets smaller.','gets bigger.','Mass does not depend on gravity.')],
 [('A 4 kg bag is on Earth, where g = 10 N/kg. Calculate its weight.','W = m × g = 4 × 10.~= 40 N.'),
  ('A boat has a 600 N forward force and 450 N of drag. Calculate the resultant force and describe the boat’s motion.','600 − 450 = 150 N.~Forwards.~The boat speeds up.'),
  ('In a crater investigation, a ball is dropped from different heights into flour. Explain why the crater gets wider as the height increases.','The ball falls further, so it gains more speed.~It has more kinetic energy when it lands.~More energy pushes more flour aside.'),
  ('Explain the difference between mass and weight, and why an astronaut weighs less on the Moon.','Mass is the amount of matter, in kg.~Weight is the force of gravity on an object, in N.~The Moon has a weaker gravitational field strength than Earth.~So the same mass has a smaller weight there.')])

U('Elements and materials',
 [('Which is the correct symbol for magnesium?','Mg.','MG.','Ma.','First capital, then lower case.'),
  ('How many atoms are in one molecule of H₂O?','3.','2.','1.','Two hydrogen and one oxygen.'),
  ('Which is a synthetic polymer?','Polythene.','Wool.','Cotton.','It is made in factories.')],
 [('Name the elements in sodium chloride and give its formula.','Sodium and chlorine.~NaCl.'),
  ('Give the number of each type of atom in Ca(OH)₂.','1 calcium.~2 oxygen.~2 hydrogen.'),
  ('Explain why copper is used for wires but plastic is used to cover them.','Copper is a good electrical conductor.~It is also ductile, so it can be drawn into wires.~Plastic is an insulator, which keeps people safe from shocks.'),
  ('Evaluate the use of plastic for drinks bottles.','Plastic is light, cheap and does not break easily.~It is waterproof and can be moulded into shapes.~However, most plastics do not biodegrade and cause litter or harm wildlife.~Recycling or reusable bottles reduce this problem.')])

U('Periodic table',
 [('A vertical column in the periodic table is a…','group.','period.','row.','Groups have similar properties.'),
  ('Which element is a noble gas?','Neon.','Sodium.','Chlorine.','Group 0.'),
  ('Going down Group 1, the metals become…','more reactive.','less reactive.','gases.','Potassium reacts faster than lithium.')],
 [('Name the group that contains the halogens, and give one example.','Group 7.~Fluorine / chlorine / bromine / iodine.'),
  ('Describe what you would see when potassium is added to water.','It floats and moves quickly across the surface.~It fizzes and melts into a ball.~It burns with a lilac flame / may make a small bang.'),
  ('Rubidium is below potassium in Group 1. Predict how it reacts with water and explain your prediction.','It reacts even more vigorously (possibly explosively).~It makes an alkaline solution and hydrogen.~Reactivity increases down Group 1.'),
  ('Compare the properties of Group 1 metals and transition metals.','Group 1 metals are soft (can be cut with a knife); transition metals are hard.~Group 1 metals have low melting points; transition metals have high melting points.~Group 1 metals are very reactive; transition metals are much less reactive.~Transition metals form coloured compounds and are used as catalysts.')])

U('Breathing and health',
 [('Gas exchange happens in the…','alveoli.','trachea.','diaphragm.','Tiny air sacs.'),
  ('When you breathe in, the diaphragm…','contracts and flattens.','relaxes and moves up.','stays still.','This increases the chest volume.'),
  ('Which substance in tobacco smoke is addictive?','Nicotine.','Tar.','Carbon dioxide.','Nicotine causes dependence.')],
 [('Name the gas that diffuses from the blood into the alveoli and the gas that diffuses the other way.','Carbon dioxide into the alveoli.~Oxygen into the blood.'),
  ('Describe what happens to the ribs, diaphragm and chest volume when you breathe out.','The intercostal muscles relax and the ribs move down and in.~The diaphragm relaxes and moves up.~The chest volume decreases, so air is pushed out.'),
  ('Explain two ways the alveoli are adapted for gas exchange.','Large surface area from millions of alveoli.~Walls one cell thick for a short diffusion distance.~Good blood supply keeps a high concentration difference.'),
  ('Explain how smoking damages health.','Tar coats the lungs and contains chemicals that cause cancer.~Smoke damages the cilia, so mucus and dirt build up, causing coughing and infections.~Carbon monoxide reduces the oxygen the blood can carry.~Nicotine raises heart rate and blood pressure, increasing the risk of heart disease.')])

U('Digestion and nutrition',
 [('Which nutrient is needed for growth and repair?','Protein.','Fibre.','Water.','Protein builds cells.'),
  ('Iodine solution turns blue-black with…','starch.','protein.','fat.','The starch test.'),
  ('Where are most small food molecules absorbed into the blood?','Small intestine.','Stomach.','Large intestine.','Villi absorb food.')],
 [('Name the enzyme that digests fats and the products formed.','Lipase.~Fatty acids and glycerol.'),
  ('Describe how to test a food sample for sugar.','Grind the food and mix with water.~Add Benedict’s solution and heat in a water bath.~A colour change from blue to brick-red shows sugar.'),
  ('Explain why the small intestine is good at absorbing food.','It is very long.~It has villi, which give a huge surface area.~The villi have thin walls and a good blood supply.'),
  ('Explain why a balanced diet is important, giving the role of three different nutrients.','A balanced diet gives the right amounts of each nutrient.~Carbohydrates (and fats) provide energy.~Protein is used for growth and repair.~Vitamins and minerals keep the body healthy; fibre keeps food moving through the gut.')])

U('Sound',
 [('Sound cannot travel through…','a vacuum.','water.','steel.','There are no particles.'),
  ('A larger amplitude makes a sound…','louder.','higher-pitched.','faster.','Amplitude affects loudness.'),
  ('The part of the ear that changes vibrations into nerve impulses is the…','cochlea.','eardrum.','ear canal.','It contains hair cells.')],
 [('A sound wave has a frequency of 500 Hz. What does this mean?','500 vibrations (waves)…~… every second.'),
  ('Thunder is heard 3 s after lightning is seen. Sound travels at 340 m/s. How far away is the storm?','Distance = speed × time.~= 340 × 3…~… = 1020 m.'),
  ('Explain why sound travels faster in solids than in gases.','Sound is passed on by vibrating particles.~In a solid, the particles are much closer together.~So vibrations are passed on more quickly.'),
  ('Describe how sound from a drum reaches and is detected by your ear.','The drum skin vibrates and makes the air particles vibrate.~The vibrations pass through the air as a longitudinal wave.~They make the eardrum vibrate, and the ossicles pass the vibrations to the cochlea.~The cochlea sends electrical impulses along the auditory nerve to the brain.')])

U('Light',
 [('The angle of reflection is equal to the…','angle of incidence.','angle of refraction.','normal.','The law of reflection.'),
  ('White light is split into colours by a…','prism.','plane mirror.','red filter.','This is dispersion.'),
  ('A red book seen in blue light looks…','black.','red.','blue.','It absorbs the blue light.')],
 [('Light travels from air into glass. Describe how its speed and direction change.','It slows down.~It bends towards the normal.'),
  ('A ray of light hits a mirror at 40° to the normal. State the angle of reflection and between which two lines it is measured, and name the law this shows.','40°.~Between the normal and the reflected ray.~The law of reflection: angle of incidence = angle of reflection.'),
  ('Explain why a white shirt looks white in sunlight.','Sunlight contains all the colours.~The white shirt reflects all of them.~All the colours together reach our eyes and look white.'),
  ('Describe an experiment to show that light travels in straight lines and reflects at equal angles.','Shine a narrow ray of light from a ray box at a plane mirror on paper.~Draw the normal and mark the incident and reflected rays.~Measure the angles of incidence and reflection with a protractor.~Repeat for different angles; the two angles are equal each time.')])

U('Reaction types',
 [('Burning a fuel in oxygen is…','combustion.','thermal decomposition.','neutralisation.','Reaction with oxygen.'),
  ('The test for carbon dioxide is…','it turns limewater milky.','a squeaky pop.','it relights a glowing splint.','Limewater goes cloudy.'),
  ('In a sealed flask, the total mass during a reaction…','stays the same.','increases.','decreases.','Atoms are conserved.')],
 [('Give two products of the complete combustion of methane.','Carbon dioxide.~Water.'),
  ('Write the word equation for the thermal decomposition of calcium carbonate.','Calcium carbonate →~calcium oxide +~carbon dioxide.'),
  ('A student heats magnesium in a crucible and the mass increases. Explain why.','The magnesium reacts with oxygen from the air.~Oxygen atoms join to form magnesium oxide.~The added oxygen makes the mass increase.'),
  ('Explain why the mass of an open beaker of acid and marble chips decreases, and how to show that mass is really conserved.','The reaction makes carbon dioxide gas.~The gas escapes into the air, so it is not weighed.~Do the reaction in a sealed container (e.g. a flask with a balloon over it).~The total mass then stays the same, showing atoms are conserved.')])

U('Chemical energy',
 [('A reaction that makes the surroundings warmer is…','exothermic.','endothermic.','reversible only.','Energy is released.'),
  ('A catalyst…','speeds up a reaction and is not used up.','is used up in the reaction.','always slows reactions.','It can be reused.'),
  ('Which is endothermic?','Thermal decomposition.','Combustion.','Neutralisation.','It takes in energy.')],
 [('The temperature of a reaction mixture falls from 21 °C to 14 °C. Calculate the change and name the type of reaction.','A decrease of 7 °C.~Endothermic.'),
  ('Give three ways to speed up the reaction between magnesium and acid.','Increase the temperature.~Use more concentrated acid.~Use powder instead of a ribbon (larger surface area) / add a catalyst.'),
  ('Explain why enzymes stop working at high temperatures.','Enzymes are proteins with a special shape.~High temperatures change their shape (denature them).~The substrate no longer fits, so the reaction is not sped up.'),
  ('Describe how to find out whether dissolving a salt in water is exothermic or endothermic.','Measure the starting temperature of water in an insulated cup.~Add the salt and stir.~Record the highest or lowest temperature reached.~A rise means exothermic; a fall means endothermic.')])

U('Respiration',
 [('Aerobic respiration happens in the…','mitochondria.','nucleus.','cell wall.','Mitochondria release energy.'),
  ('Which is a product of anaerobic respiration in muscles?','Lactic acid.','Oxygen.','Ethanol.','Lactic acid builds up.'),
  ('Yeast makes bread rise because it releases…','carbon dioxide.','oxygen.','lactic acid.','Gas bubbles get trapped.')],
 [('Write the word equation for aerobic respiration.','Glucose + oxygen →~carbon dioxide + water.'),
  ('Give three differences between aerobic and anaerobic respiration in humans.','Aerobic uses oxygen; anaerobic does not.~Aerobic releases more energy per glucose.~Aerobic makes carbon dioxide and water; anaerobic makes lactic acid.'),
  ('Explain why your breathing rate stays high for a while after a sprint.','During the sprint muscles respired anaerobically, making lactic acid.~Extra oxygen is needed to break down the lactic acid.~This is called the oxygen debt.'),
  ('A student measures her pulse before and after exercise. Describe the method and explain the result she should expect.','Measure resting pulse for 1 minute, then exercise for a set time.~Measure pulse immediately after, then every minute until it returns to normal.~The pulse rate rises during exercise.~The heart beats faster to deliver more oxygen and glucose for respiration in the muscles.')])

U('Photosynthesis',
 [('Photosynthesis happens in the…','chloroplasts.','mitochondria.','nucleus.','They contain chlorophyll.'),
  ('Which gas do plants take in for photosynthesis?','Carbon dioxide.','Oxygen.','Nitrogen.','CO₂ enters through stomata.'),
  ('Tiny pores on leaves are called…','stomata.','alveoli.','villi.','They let gases in and out.')],
 [('Write the word equation for photosynthesis.','Carbon dioxide + water →~glucose + oxygen (using light).'),
  ('Give three ways a leaf is adapted for photosynthesis.','Broad to absorb lots of light.~Thin so gases diffuse a short distance.~Contains many chloroplasts / has stomata for gas exchange.'),
  ('Pondweed gives 30 bubbles per minute with a lamp 10 cm away and 8 bubbles per minute at 40 cm. Explain the result.','Closer lamp means more light intensity.~More light gives a faster rate of photosynthesis.~So more oxygen bubbles are produced.'),
  ('Describe how to test a leaf for starch and explain why this shows photosynthesis has happened.','Boil the leaf in water to kill it, then in ethanol (heated in a water bath) to remove chlorophyll.~Rinse and spread on a tile.~Add iodine: blue-black shows starch.~Glucose made by photosynthesis is stored as starch.')])

U('Working scientifically investigations',
 [('A test is fair when…','only the independent variable is changed.','all variables are changed.','only one result is taken.','Control variables stay the same.'),
  ('Terminal velocity is reached when…','air resistance equals weight.','weight is zero.','air resistance is zero.','Forces balance.'),
  ('Conduction is fastest in…','metals.','plastics.','air.','Metals have free electrons.')],
 [('A cord is 30 cm long and stretches to 38 cm. Calculate the extension.','38 − 30…~… = 8 cm.'),
  ('In a bouncing-ball test, give three control variables.','The same ball.~The same surface.~The same way of releasing the ball (no throwing) / same temperature.'),
  ('A student times how long salt takes to dissolve at different temperatures. Suggest one improvement and explain how it helps.','Use a data logger/conductivity meter or a black background to judge the end point.~This makes the end point less subjective.~So the times are more accurate and repeatable.'),
  ('Plan an investigation into how the mass of a paper case affects its falling time.','Change mass by stacking cases (1–5 cases).~Drop from the same height each time.~Time the fall with a stopwatch and repeat three times to find a mean.~Plot mass against mean time and look for a pattern.')])
